  

<h2 id="its-not-really-about-the-app">It’s not really about the app</h2>
<p>When <a href="https://mastodon.bsd.cafe/@stefano/116177568965310505" target="_blank">Stefano Marinelli posted about compromises and tolerance</a>, I knew he was right because I’ve seen this in action:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Life is full of compromises. And the more you live, the more you learn (or should learn) to be at least a little tolerant of the choices others make, even when they seem to clash with your own convictions. …Fight your battles. Stand for what you believe in. But try to remain clear-headed. Life teaches that you often go farther by moving slowly but steadily toward your destination, stopping every now and then to look around.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Stefano was speaking in the context of Windows 11 and Linux, but the principle behind his words is quite profound and far-reaching.</p>
<p>I’ve come to see that some choices fall into the realm of<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>ideal’ or<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>not ideal’ rather than<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>right’ or<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>wrong’. There are no moral absolutes when it comes to the kind of digital setup that works for you.</p>
<p>We can save a lot of internal angst by choosing to focus on the destination rather than obsessing on the best way to get there.</p>
<p>Recently I advised someone to continue keeping Word docs on their desktop rather than organising them elsewhere. In no world is that a good long-term strategy, but their current system is working (for now) and any major changes at this point would lead to them feeling overwhelmed. One step at a time!</p>
<h3 id="here-let-me-fix-that-for-you">Here, let me fix that for you…</h3>
<p>It’s usually easier to recognise rigid attitudes in others than in ourselves. Responding strongly when others<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>get it wrong’ might be an indication that we would benefit from taking a walk around the topic to see what it looks like from other perspectives.</p>
<p>Take the topic of using open source apps. There’s a <em>lot</em> of strong feelings around about that, with not a small number sincerely believing that, unless an app’s code base is available to users, they will be locked into that app for all time.</p>
<p><a href="https://social.tchncs.de/@yngmar/116635142702094411" target="_blank">@yngmar@social.tchncs.de</a> says they are<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>looking forward to the great howling and teeth gnashing when the incomprehensibly popular Obsidian<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>updates’ their business model and everyone can experience how to port their oh so portable markdown notes into another thing.” They believe we must<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>use open source or risk being blackmailed later.”</p>
<p>I felt the<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>focus on the destination” principle clarify in my mind as I wrote the following reply:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Open source is an attitude, not an app. I happily use Obsidian with my<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>open source’ gloves on.</p>
<p>Nothing in my vault is not immediately and perfectly readable by a swag of other Markdown apps, open source or not. If it doesn’t work in any text editor, forget it. The Obsidian queries I rely on could be swapped for regex in an afternoon.</p>
<p>Why not drop the anti-Obsidian rhetoric and focus on<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>open source attitude” evangelism instead.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So what exactly is an<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>open source attitude”?</p>
<p>It’s a way of approaching the creation, storage and retrieval of digital information in a manner that does not lock you in to any one app.</p>
<p>In simple terms: You made a Thing in app <em>A</em>, but you don’t need that app in order to access and work with your Thing. This is because app <em>A</em> stores data in a format accessible by many other apps, or it has good export options (and, key point, you used them).</p>
<h3 id="how-to-cultivate-digital-freedom">How to cultivate digital freedom</h3>
<p>A couple of years ago I compiled the following list of <em>Rules for Responsible Knowledge Management</em>.</p>
<ol type="1">
<li>Keep your data in a portable format (eg. <code>.txt</code>, <code>.md</code>, <code>.pdf</code>)</li>
<li>Store your data locally (on your own machine, not solely in the cloud)</li>
<li>Always have at least one backup</li>
<li>Don’t rely on features that only work in one app</li>
<li>Beware of apps that make it hard to leave</li>
<li>When using a gatekeeper<a href="#footnote-1LA0" class="footnote-ref" id="ref-1LA0" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a> app, make data export a ritual you never miss</li>
<li>Don’t be dogmatic about plain text when working with people who don’t yet get it</li>
<li>Put fleeting notes wherever you like (but in 3 or fewer places)</li>
<li>Use the right tool for the job</li>
<li>Don’t use a digital tool in situations where pen and paper is a better choice</li>
</ol>
<p>How are you doing with those? Are there any you would add, or change? Consider choosing one you are weak in and do something about it this week. Bit by bit you can cultivate digital freedom, treating apps as a lens through which to view and shape your (highly portable) work.</p>
<p>And remember, sometimes it’s fine to make a less than ideal choice as long as you are working within your current limitations to get where you want to be.</p>
